 and keeps options open.\n\nReason:  \nDeveloping the knight to f6 is a core opening principle — it increases board control, supports future central breaks like ...d5 or ...e4, and improves piece activity. With White already having played Nc3 (knight on c3, implied by NWF1 on c3), Black must respond dynamically. Nf6 challenges the center and avoids falling behind in development.\n\nAnswer: N: g8-f6'}
